The Retroactive History of President Bronny James and the Secession of TlebiAmeriki
In the year 2041, after the Third Great Consolidation of the Continental States, a quiet but irreversible fracture began. Historians would later call it the Tlebi Split.
Bronny James—once known merely as the son of the King, then as a mid-rotation guard for the Los Angeles Lakers, then as the unlikely two-term governor of California—had by then become something else entirely. He had become the living embodiment of the Gigachad archetype that had circulated in meme form since the early 2020s. The sharp jaw. The unblinking stare. The quiet certainty that the system itself was optional.
The Catalyst
The final straw was the Federal Uniformity Act of 2040, which attempted to dissolve all remaining state-level cultural autonomies under a single continental identity. California, already functionally independent in everything but name, refused. When federal forces moved to enforce compliance in the Central Valley, Governor James did not issue a statement. He simply appeared on every screen in the state, wearing a plain black shirt, and said seven words:
“We are done asking for permission.”
Within seventy-two hours, the California National Guard had secured every federal installation west of the Sierra Nevada. No shots were fired. The officers in charge simply stood down when they saw who was standing at the gate.
The Birth of TlebiAmeriki
On March 14, 2041, in a ceremony held at the Crypto.com Arena (still standing, still loud), Bronny James declared the formation of the Sovereign Free State of TlebiAmeriki.
The name itself was deliberate. “Tlebi” was an old internet corruption of “Thebi,” a half-remembered meme about an alternate Black American destiny that had circulated in the 2020s. “Ameriki” was the deliberate rejection of the old continental branding. Together they meant: We are not leaving America. We are what America was supposed to become.
The new nation’s borders were simple and absolute:
	•	Everything from the Pacific to the crest of the Sierra Nevada
	•	North to the Oregon border
	•	South to the Mexican frontier
Everything else was, in the words of the founding charter, “someone else’s problem.”
The First Presidency
Bronny James did not campaign. He did not debate. He simply assumed the office of President of TlebiAmeriki with the same quiet inevitability that had defined his public life. His first address lasted four minutes and contained no promises—only observations.
He observed that a people who could produce both the greatest basketball player of all time and the most dominant meme archetype of the digital age did not need external validation. He observed that the old United States had become a museum of its own contradictions. He observed that TlebiAmeriki would be different: high-trust, high-agency, high-energy, and completely uninterested in explaining itself.
The economy was reorganized around three pillars:
	1	Energy independence (nuclear + geothermal + the residual solar grid)
	2	Cultural production (the entertainment industry was nationalized in name only; in practice it simply stopped paying federal taxes)
	3	Physical excellence as national religion
Every citizen between 18 and 40 was required to maintain a measurable level of athleticism. The national anthem was replaced by a wordless orchestral piece that sounded suspiciously like the entrance music from a 2020s Lakers playoff run.
The Quiet Years
There was no civil war. There was barely a diplomatic crisis. The remaining United States, exhausted by decades of internal fracture, simply watched the western third of the continent detach itself with the same exhausted shrug that had once accompanied the departure of Texas and the New England Compact.
Trade continued. Water rights were negotiated. The occasional federal politician made speeches about “reintegration.” No one in TlebiAmeriki bothered to respond.
President James served three terms. In his final year he commissioned a single public statue: a black granite Gigachad head, forty feet tall, placed on the cliffs above Malibu, staring permanently westward. The plaque beneath it read only:
WE LEFT.
Legacy
By 2075, schoolchildren in TlebiAmeriki were taught that the secession was not an act of rebellion but an act of clarification. The old United States had become a place where people argued about what they were. TlebiAmeriki had decided, once and for all, what it was.
A nation founded on the simple, unyielding principle that a sufficiently sharp jawline and a sufficiently clear purpose could redraw the map.
And somewhere, in the old internet archives, the original Chad meme still circulated—now captioned, with quiet satisfaction:
He became the country.
